# Research portfolio: reducing preventable readmissions without increasing bedside nurse workload

## Design premise

The central hypothesis is that readmission prevention should be shifted from “more work during the index hospitalization” to:

1. automated detection of unresolved transition risks;
2. task delegation to pharmacists, community health workers, navigators, and centralized teams;
3. asynchronous post-discharge support;
4. better targeting of interventions by modifiable risk, not merely predicted risk.

This is feasible because nurse-led transitional care can reduce longer-term readmissions, but existing interventions often rely heavily on telephone follow-up and nursing labor. Evidence also supports community health workers and pharmacists as alternative delivery channels. citeturn0search1 citeturn1search3 citeturn1search4

AHRQ emphasizes that discharge errors, medication problems, unclear instructions, and fragmented follow-up are common targets for intervention. citeturn0search2

